{'id': 97, 'role': 'tool', 'content': '{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc", "description": "Use when solving Continual-ARC puzzles.", "tags": [], "related_skills": [], "content": "---\\nname: continual-arc\\ndescription: Use when solving Continual-ARC puzzles.\\n---\\n\\n# Continual-ARC stream solving\\n\\nApply to ARC-style grid tasks delivered as separate stream instances with a persistent per-task Python transform and exactly one JSON action per response.\\n\\n## Required workflow\\n\\n1. Check the exact task-ID file under `workspace/programs/<task id>.py` before analysis or demos. If present, run its `transform(grid)` on the test input and submit that result first. Never replace an existing solution with an identity stub.\\n2. If no program exists or its prior answer was wrong, inspect the complete grid and identify background, objects, shapes, colors, relative positions, and plausible transformations. Distinguish visual hypotheses from evidence.\\n3. Request demonstrations only when they can resolve meaningful ambiguity; each request costs score. Focus on what a pair will disambiguate: object correspondence, copy anchor, rotation/reflection, colors, clipping, or overlaps.\\n4. Implement a general `transform(grid)`. Reason explicitly about object identity, template membership, anchor convention (top-left, center, colored marker), and whether source cells are retained, recolored, or overwritten.\\n5. Execute the program on every available demo input and compare the complete output grid to each provided output. Do not submit until every pair matches. Then run on the current test, verify dimensions/cell values, and submit only the program-produced grid.\\n6. After the verdict, persist the validated transform at the exact task path, replacing older code as appropriate. Once the instance is over, send `finish` separately.\\n\\n## Technical guidance\\n\\n- Most-frequent color can suggest background, but verify via object structure; background is not necessarily zero.\\n- Connected components help locate objects, but touching colors can merge, and logical motifs may be disconnected. Compare 4- and 8-neighbor components if interpretation changes.\\n- Use explicit bounding boxes and offsets; center and top-left anchors are not interchangeable, especially for even dimensions.\\n- Define collision and clipping behavior from demonstrations, not incidental loop order. Preserve original grid cells unless the transformation establishes otherwise.\\n- Regression-check full grids, not selected cells or a plausible visual imp', 'tc': None, 'tool_name': 'skill_view'}
